Translation patch: EdwardsPoint::compress_batch_alloc (src/edwards.rs)

Change: `inputs.iter().map(|input| input.Z).collect()` and
`inputs.iter().zip(&zs).map(|(input, recip)| { .. }).collect()` become two forward index
loops pushing into `Vec`s; the second loop body computes the same
`AffinePoint { x: &inputs[i].X * &zs[i], y: &inputs[i].Y * &zs[i] }.compress()`.

Why: the Aeneas Lean library models only `Iterator::next`; the provided methods
(`map`, `zip`, `fold`, `filter`, `collect`, `rev`, `skip`, `size_hint`, `by_ref`, ...)
are missing (AeneasVerif/aeneas#1057), so the original does not build in Lean.

Behaviour: identical. Both loops visit `inputs[0..n]` in order and perform the same operations.
`zs` is built with length `n` and keeps it through `invert_batch_alloc` (see below), so the
`zip` never truncated and `zs[i]` is in bounds; `i += 1` with `i < n` cannot overflow. Loop
bounds depend only on the public length.

Facts relied on (not visible in the diff): `FieldElement::invert_batch_alloc` takes `&mut [FieldElement]` (the `Vec` derefs to a
slice), so it cannot change the length of the vector it inverts.

Remove this patch when Aeneas translates the original code: delete the file,
run ./scripts/aeneas-translate.sh and `lake build`, and check both succeed.

